  padding: 1rem;
  background-color: #f3f5f8;
}
`
			},
			checks: [
				{
					id: 'plan-section',
					kind: 'exists',
					selector: '#webhook-plan'
				},
				{
					id: 'no-blanks',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#webhook-plan',
					matches: '^[^…]*$'
				},
				{
					id: 'channel',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#channel',
					matches: 'Slack|Telegram|WhatsApp|Signal|Discord|Teams|[Ee]-?mail|SMS|chat|push'
				},
				{
					id: 'hmac',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#handler',
					matches: 'createHmac\\(\\s*[\'"]sha256[\'"]'
				},
				{
					id: 'secret-from-env',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#handler',
					matches: 'process\\.env\\.\\w+'
				},
				{
					id: 'no-hardcoded-secret',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#handler',
					matches: '^(?![\\s\\S]*(?:SECRET|secret|TOKEN|token|KEY|key)\\s*=\\s*[\'"][^\'"]{6,}[\'"])'
				},
				{
					id: 'safe-compare',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#handler',
					matches: 'timingSafeEqual'
				},
				{
					id: 'rejects-bad-signature',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#handler',
					matches: 'status\\(\\s*40[13]\\s*\\)|statusCode\\s*=\\s*40[13]'
				},
				{
					id: 'idempotent',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#handler',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:event\\.id|delivery|idempot))(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:\\.has\\(|already|processed|seen|SELECT))'
				},
				{
					id: 'fast-ack',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#handler',
					matches: 'status\\(\\s*200\\s*\\)|statusCode\\s*=\\s*200|sendStatus\\(\\s*200\\s*\\)'
				},
				{
					id: 'retry-answer',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#retry',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:retr(?:y|ies)|again|repeat|resend|redeliver))(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:queue|log|not lost|won’t be lost|idempoten))'
				},
				{
					id: 'graphql-vs-rest',
					kind: 'text',
					selector: '#graphql',
					matches: '^(?=[\\s\\S]*GraphQL)(?=[\\s\\S]*REST)(?=[\\s\\S]*(?:if|when|because|since))'
				},
				{
					id: 'graphql-names-item',
					kind: 'field',
					selector: '#graphql',
					field: 'items[].title',
					min: 1,
					when: {
						brief: 'items[].title'
					}
				}
			]
		},
		quiz: [
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'How does a webhook differ from polling?',
				options: [
					'With polling you ask “any news?” every few minutes yourself, while a webhook comes to you with a request when the event happens',
					'A webhook works only in a browser',
					'Polling is faster, because it does not use the network'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Polling is wasteful (the answer is mostly “nothing new”) and always late. A webhook reverses the direction: the service where the event happened sends a POST to your address.',
					'No: a webhook is an ordinary HTTP request between servers, and no browser takes part.',
					'No: polling is exactly what sends many empty requests over the network.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Why is the signature of an incoming webhook checked?',
				options: [
					'To make sure the real service sent the request and the body was not changed on the way',
					'To speed up processing',
					'To count the inquiries'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Anyone can find out the address of your receiver. Only someone who knows the secret can make the signature (an HMAC of the body with a shared secret), so a forged request is cut off before processing.',
					'No: the check adds work, but it protects against forgery.',
					'No: the signature has nothing to do with counting.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'Your handler did not answer in time, and the service sent the same event a second time. What should a correct handler do?',
				options: [
					'Recognize the event by its id and not process it again: the processing is idempotent',
					'Process it a second time: it may come in handy',
					'Block the service for good'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. Webhooks are delivered “at least once”, so repeats come from the service and from failures alike. If you remember the ids of processed events, the owner will not get the inquiry twice.',
					'No: the owner would get two identical inquiries, and a payment would be charged twice.',
					'No: a repeat shows that the service is doing what it should.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'multiple',
				prompt: 'What is true about storing the secret used to check signatures?',
				options: [
					'It goes in environment variables or a secrets store',
					'It must not be written in public code or a repository',
					'It can be posted in the README so it isn’t lost',
					'It is changed if it may have leaked'
				],
				answer: [0, 1, 3],
				why: [
					'Yes: many people see the code, while only the server sees its environment variables.',
					'Yes: whatever lands in a public repository is considered leaked for good.',
					'No: everybody reads the README, and the signature would no longer guarantee anything.',
					'Yes: rotating a secret is ordinary hygiene.'
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'match',
				prompt: 'Match each approach with its description',
				pairs: [
					['REST', 'many addresses, each returning its resource whole'],
					['GraphQL', 'one address, and the client lists the fields it needs'],
					['webhook', 'the service sends a request about an event to your address'],
					['HMAC signature', 'a hash of the body with a shared secret: it proves the request is genuine']
				]
			},
			{
				type: 'single',
				prompt: 'For a list of three services on a small business site, which is more sensible, REST or GraphQL?',
				options: [
					'REST: the requests are simple and the same each time, and GraphQL would add complexity with no gain',
					'GraphQL: it is always better',
					'Neither: a list needs no API at all'
				],
				answer: 0,
				why: [
					'Yes. GraphQL pays off when different clients need different fields, or when there are many related resources (a mobile app and a website). For three services there is nothing to gain, and the cost in complexity and caching is real.',
					'No: it has a cost. It is harder to cache, you must guard against queries that are too heavy, and you need a query layer of its own.',
					'No: if the list changes without re-uploading the site, it needs an API, however simple.'
				]
			}
		],
		cards: [
			{
				id: 'server-webhook',
				front: 'What is a webhook?',
				back: 'A callback over HTTP: when something happens in a service (a payment, an inquiry, a commit), it sends a POST describing the event to your address, so you needn’t poll it.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-webhook-signature',
				front: 'How do you check that a webhook is genuine?',
				back: 'The service signs the raw request body with a shared secret (HMAC SHA-256) and sends the signature in a header. The receiver computes its own and compares them with a safe function. No match: 401, and the event is not processed.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-idempotent-webhook',
				front: 'Why must webhook processing be idempotent?',
				back: 'Delivery is “at least once”: after failures the service repeats the request. Remember event ids and do not process a repeat. Answer 200 quickly, and do heavy work in a queue.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-graphql',
				front: 'What is GraphQL?',
				back: 'A query language for APIs (Facebook, 2015): one address, a typed schema, and the client lists the fields and related data it needs. No surplus fields and no extra requests, but caching and protection get harder.'
			},
			{
				id: 'server-graphql-or-rest',
				front: 'When GraphQL, and when REST?',
				back: 'REST: simple, similar requests, caching, small sites. GraphQL: many different clients (a site, a mobile app) and a lot of related data, and a team ready to support it.'
			}
		],
		sources: [
			{
				title: 'GraphQL: Introduction to GraphQL',
				url: 'https://graphql.org/learn/',
				licence: 'CC-BY',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'Stripe: Webhooks',
				url: 'https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'GitHub Docs: Validating webhook deliveries',
				url: 'https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks/validating-webhook-deliveries',
				licence: 'CC-BY',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'Slack: Sending messages using incoming webhooks',
				url: 'https://docs.slack.dev/messaging/sending-messages-using-incoming-webhooks/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'ICO: International transfers',
				url: 'https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/international-transfers/',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			},
			{
				title: 'Telegram Bot API: setWebhook',
				url: 'https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#setwebhook',
				licence: 'link-only',
				kind: 'docs',
				lang: 'en'
			}
		],
		videos: [
			{
				title: 'What Is GraphQL? REST vs. GraphQL (ByteByteGo)',
				url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yWzKJPw_VzM',
				licence: 'proprietary-link',
				kind: 'video',
				lang: 'en'
			}
		],
		guide: {
			intro: 'You’ll set up the path of an inquiry: the form, then a webhook, then the owner. The receiver’s code is written as for a real server, and you can try the signature on the diagram above. The key ideas of the lesson: the signature, a secret kept out of the code, and repeats.',
			hints: [
				'The first line: import crypto from node:crypto. The secret: const SECRET = process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET (do not write the value of the secret into the code).',
				'The signature: crypto.createHmac with sha256 and SECRET, then update with req.rawBody, then digest as hex. Compare with crypto.timingSafeEqual on two Buffers. No match: res.status(401).end().',
				'Repeats: const seen = new Set(), and if (seen.has(event.id)) return res.status(200).end(), then seen.add(event.id). At the end, res.status(200).end().',
				'In the paragraphs: the channel (Slack or email), what happens on a failure (retry, queue, idempotency), and the GraphQL versus REST comparison on one of your items, with an “if…” in it.'
			],
			mistakes: {
				'plan-section': 'The “Inquiry, webhook, owner” block (the section with the id webhook-plan) is gone. Put it back.',
				'no-blanks': 'There are still “…” left. Replace every one.',
				channel: 'Name the delivery channel: Slack, email or another.',
				hmac: 'The signature is an HMAC SHA-256: crypto.createHmac with sha256 and SECRET.',
